WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.810
Working for the SCP Foundation is no walk
in the park.

00:00:03.810 --> 00:00:08.610
You’re forced to remain under a veil of
secrecy at all times, risking your life every

00:00:08.610 --> 00:00:15.740
single day contending with dangerous and inexplicable
anomalies, and sometimes, you have to do things

00:00:15.740 --> 00:00:17.290
that you’re not proud of.

00:00:17.290 --> 00:00:22.619
Whether it’s forcing others to collect the
dangerous Y-909 compound from SCP-3000, the

00:00:22.619 --> 00:00:27.810
giant eel responsible for creating the chemicals
in amnestics, or engaging in a variety of

00:00:27.810 --> 00:00:34.019
innumerable unscrupulous activities all done
in service of maintaining normalcy, the nature

00:00:34.019 --> 00:00:39.460
of work at the Foundation invites difficult
problems with no clear solutions.

00:00:39.460 --> 00:00:44.170
Moral dilemmas challenging enough to give
a philosopher an aneurysm and enough trauma

00:00:44.170 --> 00:00:49.449
that amnestics became standard issue tools
in a Foundation personnel’s arsenal.

00:00:49.449 --> 00:00:54.760
Not just to wipe the minds of others, but
also to use on themselves, allowing their

00:00:54.760 --> 00:01:00.620
mental states a reprieve from the atrocities
they may or may not have committed.

00:01:00.620 --> 00:01:02.850
But that’s part of the job description.

00:01:02.850 --> 00:01:07.650
You know what you’re signing up for when
it comes to the SCP Foundation, even if you’re

00:01:07.650 --> 00:01:09.869
unsure of what your job will entail.

00:01:09.869 --> 00:01:14.729
What you need to understand is that when you
work for the SCP Foundation, every day could

00:01:14.729 --> 00:01:21.060
potentially be your last, and if it’s not
yours, it’s almost definitely someone else’s.

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:24.700
Maybe that’s what the recruiting officers
tell prospective researchers and agents to

00:01:24.700 --> 00:01:26.170
really cheer them up!

00:01:26.170 --> 00:01:30.450
Sometimes, carrying out the Foundation’s
morally questionable acts results in - or

00:01:30.450 --> 00:01:38.320
in some cases - requires one of the most irreversible,
permanent actions: taking a life.

00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:41.579
Death is a common sight at the Foundation.

00:01:41.579 --> 00:01:46.700
Your job is constantly at risk of prematurely
disappearing due to one faulty containment

00:01:46.700 --> 00:01:51.500
chamber leading to a full-scale breach, or
just having the bad luck that comes with being

00:01:51.500 --> 00:01:56.460
one of the first ones to explore a mysterious
location before all of the anomalous effects

00:01:56.460 --> 00:01:58.460
within are mapped out.

00:01:58.460 --> 00:02:02.969
Being assigned to a dangerous anomaly will
practically ensure you’ll see death, and

00:02:02.969 --> 00:02:05.049
potentially be the cause of it.

00:02:05.049 --> 00:02:10.210
D-Class personnel are the Foundation’s lab
rats, reportedly sourced from death row inmates

00:02:10.210 --> 00:02:16.550
and other unsavory characters; these guys
do the jobs that no one else wants to do.

00:02:16.550 --> 00:02:22.220
Whether it’s cleaning the containment chamber
of a hostile, neck-snapping SCP-173, or venturing

00:02:22.220 --> 00:02:28.570
into the dark, unending depths of SCP-087’s
staircases, there is no shortage of incredibly

00:02:28.570 --> 00:02:31.940
dangerous tasks for the Foundation to hand
out.

00:02:31.940 --> 00:02:36.360
With so much death and destruction surrounding
them, It shouldn’t come as a surprise that

00:02:36.360 --> 00:02:41.970
the Foundation initiated Project Dammerung
to research what happens after death, if anything.

00:02:41.970 --> 00:02:46.630
But that’s another story entirely, let’s
keep talking about D-Class personnel.

00:02:46.630 --> 00:02:51.610
There’s always someone behind the D-Class,
the one giving orders.

00:02:51.610 --> 00:02:56.580
And when those orders result in the loss of
life, it’s going to weigh down on that person’s

00:02:56.580 --> 00:02:59.500
consciousness, death row inmate or not.

00:02:59.500 --> 00:03:02.990
Sometimes amnestics are enough to smooth it
over, sometimes not.

00:03:02.990 --> 00:03:08.190
But no one can say that the Foundation does
not have an insensitivity to death.

00:03:08.190 --> 00:03:13.370
It only takes one look at an SCP file’s
cold, clinical delivery of a brutal experiment

00:03:13.370 --> 00:03:15.890
gone wrong to realize this.

00:03:15.890 --> 00:03:20.690
Names listed, names redacted, sometimes it
really feels like all these deaths are just

00:03:20.690 --> 00:03:24.200
numbers and statistics for the Foundation
to report.

00:03:24.200 --> 00:03:27.420
Maybe the “D” really does stand for disposable.

00:03:27.420 --> 00:03:29.460
On one hand, it’s understandable.

00:03:29.460 --> 00:03:34.180
The Foundation is an institution of science,
and the need for reports to be as accurate,

00:03:34.180 --> 00:03:38.730
detailed, and professional as possible outweighs
the sorrow and sadness that comes with the

00:03:38.730 --> 00:03:44.220
loss of life, but on the other hand, would
it hurt these guys to show some humanity for

00:03:44.220 --> 00:03:45.220
once?

00:03:45.220 --> 00:03:48.540
It’s always a crowd-pleaser when the Foundation
comes down from its high horse and goes the

00:03:48.540 --> 00:03:51.120
extra mile to show how much they care.

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:57.040
Like when the legendary D-14134 single-handedly
saved the world from the deadly SCP-1983,

00:03:57.040 --> 00:04:03.320
a barn house infested with vicious, heart-collecting
entities that even the Foundation had difficulty

00:04:03.320 --> 00:04:04.370
dealing with.

00:04:04.370 --> 00:04:10.870
Sure, D-14134 died, but he was posthumously
awarded the Foundation Star for his efforts!

00:04:10.870 --> 00:04:13.430
The second ever D-Class to do so!

00:04:13.430 --> 00:04:17.709
Okay, maybe the Foundation needs a bit of
work on figuring out how best to honor its

00:04:17.709 --> 00:04:19.350
most respected members.

00:04:19.350 --> 00:04:23.820
But is this cold, detached outlook just a
result of years of exposure to the hardships

00:04:23.820 --> 00:04:28.530
of anomalous containment, or is there something
larger at play?

00:04:28.530 --> 00:04:32.970
This SCP-001 proposal just may provide your
answer.

00:04:32.970 --> 00:04:37.820
As we’ve explained before, there is no single
SCP-001, but many.

00:04:37.820 --> 00:04:42.800
An old Foundation legend passed down between
personnel says that many of the highly-classified

00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:51.280
001 files are fake, mere diversions to confuse
a true 001, a file so secretive that the Foundation

00:04:51.280 --> 00:04:56.120
resorted to deception on a scale untold just
to hide it’s existence.

00:04:56.120 --> 00:04:58.150
But that’s just a rumor.

00:04:58.150 --> 00:05:04.240
The truth is that the 001 database is composed
of dozens of proposals, all of which detail

00:05:04.240 --> 00:05:07.940
objects of high importance and standing to
the Foundation at large.

00:05:07.940 --> 00:05:12.560
From Tufto’s Proposal, which details the
surprising history behind the threshold between

00:05:12.560 --> 00:05:17.910
antiquity and modernity, the Scarlet King,
to Ball’s Proposal, which focuses around

00:05:17.910 --> 00:05:23.190
a Sheaf of Papers with the capability to create
anomalies, these files don’t shy away from

00:05:23.190 --> 00:05:27.130
covering big questions and delivering even
bigger answers.

00:05:27.130 --> 00:05:31.870
But as always, keep in mind that due to the
inherent mystery and veil of secrecy involved

00:05:31.870 --> 00:05:39.430
in an SCP-001 file, not everything may be
true, or appear as simple as it seems.

00:05:39.430 --> 00:05:45.360
The anomaly we’ll be looking at today is
Solvex’s SCP-001 Proposal, which on the

00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:48.440
onset, looks deceptively simple.

00:05:48.440 --> 00:05:53.600
SCP-001 is an antique lamp, like one you’d
see at a pawn shop, or in your grandparent’s

00:05:53.600 --> 00:05:54.600
attic.

00:05:54.600 --> 00:05:59.540
While the material of the lamp itself is decidedly
not anomalous, the fuel space is filled with

00:05:59.540 --> 00:06:03.070
class-A ectomorphs, an anomalous particle.

00:06:03.070 --> 00:06:07.010
Foundation researchers have hypothesized that
the presence of these particles allow for

00:06:07.010 --> 00:06:12.020
the lamp’s flames to burn indefinitely,
and an experiment testing these capabilities

00:06:12.020 --> 00:06:16.140
lasted for over a month with no signs of extinction.

00:06:16.140 --> 00:06:21.840
Viewing its flames triggers an anomalous effect,
the suppression of responsibility for deaths

00:06:21.840 --> 00:06:23.700
caused by the viewer.

00:06:23.700 --> 00:06:30.450
That’s right, SCP-001 extinguishes responsibility
for your actions, specifically those that

00:06:30.450 --> 00:06:32.610
have resulted in the deaths of others.

00:06:32.610 --> 00:06:38.460
While your conscience may be lightened, this
object acts only as a distraction, the memory

00:06:38.460 --> 00:06:40.500
of the deaths still exist.

00:06:40.500 --> 00:06:45.940
If mentioning such a death absolutely cannot
be avoided, viewers of SCP-001 will instead

00:06:45.940 --> 00:06:48.300
describe it with a euphemism.

00:06:48.300 --> 00:06:53.380
Ever wonder why the Foundation uses the terms
‘neutralized’ or ‘terminated’ so much?

00:06:53.380 --> 00:06:58.699
That’s because SCP-001 is required viewing
for members of the Foundation.

00:06:58.699 --> 00:07:05.330
Initially, following the item’s retrieval
in Texas, SCP-001 was cataloged as a simple

00:07:05.330 --> 00:07:07.630
anomaly, a light that did not burn out.

00:07:07.630 --> 00:07:12.740
It’s secondary effect of alleviating responsibility
was discovered after the fact, no doubt after

00:07:12.740 --> 00:07:17.060
some researchers felt unusually calm about
sending a few individuals to their deaths

00:07:17.060 --> 00:07:22.139
- sorry I meant “terminations” - in service
of the Foundation one day.

00:07:22.139 --> 00:07:28.180
Its effects transfer even to pictures and
videos of SCP-001’s flames, which are distributed

00:07:28.180 --> 00:07:33.540
to all Foundation personnel under a directive
referred to as the Acquittal Protocol.

00:07:33.540 --> 00:07:39.610
Discovering SCP-001 was truly beneficial to
the early Foundation, whose personnel understandably

00:07:39.610 --> 00:07:43.430
struggled with having to take lives and make
hard decisions.

00:07:43.430 --> 00:07:47.360
Before the discovery of this object, those
working for organizations investigating the

00:07:47.360 --> 00:07:51.580
anomalous had to live with untold atrocities
weighing down on them.

00:07:51.580 --> 00:07:57.080
Take for example another SCP-001 proposal,
The Factory.

00:07:57.080 --> 00:08:01.650
One of the many conflicting, varying origin
stories for the Foundation, the Factory was

00:08:01.650 --> 00:08:06.990
one of the first anomalies investigated by
the Foundation, before it had even been established

00:08:06.990 --> 00:08:08.479
as the Foundation.

00:08:08.479 --> 00:08:13.900
A horrific industrial factory, filled to the
brim with tortured workers, burning machines,

00:08:13.900 --> 00:08:15.139
and the stench of death.

00:08:15.139 --> 00:08:19.511
While the Factory was dealt with to the best
of the early Foundation’s abilities, those

00:08:19.511 --> 00:08:23.490
involved in the operation were scarred, and
rightfully so.

00:08:23.490 --> 00:08:28.979
Perhaps to older generations of Foundation
employees who lived through horrors unimaginable,

00:08:28.979 --> 00:08:34.329
exposing the entire organization to this SCP-001
Lamp was the right thing to do.

00:08:34.329 --> 00:08:39.529
To spare the future generations of Foundation
workers from having to live with the consequences

00:08:39.529 --> 00:08:41.169
of their actions.

00:08:41.169 --> 00:08:46.220
But in doing so, in utilizing an anomaly on
such a large group of people against their

00:08:46.220 --> 00:08:50.639
will, what is the Foundation robbing from
the people who work for it?

00:08:50.639 --> 00:08:58.110
As Overseer O5-1 once described it in a correspondence
letter regarding SCP-001, “I still regret

00:08:58.110 --> 00:09:03.930
that people who join us to protect mankind
from abnormalities are changed into abnormalities

00:09:03.930 --> 00:09:05.800
and contained as such.

00:09:05.800 --> 00:09:10.480
But still we must keep going until we find
a better way than this method.

00:09:10.480 --> 00:09:12.379
We must always do our best.”

00:09:12.379 --> 00:09:18.100
In a way, the transformation of the very morality
of a human being is an anomaly in itself.

00:09:18.100 --> 00:09:22.540
By negating the effects of witnessing death,
you deliberately create behavior that goes

00:09:22.540 --> 00:09:26.499
against hundreds of thousands of years of
biological wiring.

00:09:26.499 --> 00:09:30.509
Those exposed to SCP-001 are changed fundamentally.

00:09:30.509 --> 00:09:34.490
Metaphorically, this is the curse of the Foundation
researcher.

00:09:34.490 --> 00:09:40.510
Sworn to protect, sworn to die in the dark
in silence, so that others may live in the

00:09:40.510 --> 00:09:41.510
light.

00:09:41.510 --> 00:09:45.160
And like all vows of secrecy, it comes with
a cost.

00:09:45.160 --> 00:09:49.540
It shouldn’t come as a surprise that a large
number of Foundation personnel work with the

00:09:49.540 --> 00:09:52.449
organization as a generational calling.

00:09:52.449 --> 00:09:54.700
Take the Bright family for instance.

00:09:54.700 --> 00:09:58.970
Once you’re in, there’s no getting out,
and it’s a lot easier to live as a family

00:09:58.970 --> 00:10:03.250
with knowledge of the Foundation than someone’s
parent attempting to balance a double life

00:10:03.250 --> 00:10:05.129
as a senior researcher.

00:10:05.129 --> 00:10:09.329
For some members of the Foundation, they were
thrown into this life before they were even

00:10:09.329 --> 00:10:15.759
born, and anomalies like SCP-001 only cement
the line that divides an ordinary person from

00:10:15.759 --> 00:10:18.329
those working behind the Veil.

00:10:18.329 --> 00:10:24.540
The Foundation secures, contains, and protects,
but the people making up the massive organization

00:10:24.540 --> 00:10:26.829
are just that - people.

00:10:26.829 --> 00:10:31.699
By furthering the divide between the general
public and a member of the SCP Foundation,

00:10:31.699 --> 00:10:34.819
you only ensure what O5-1 already knew.

00:10:34.819 --> 00:10:36.029
He continued:

00:10:36.029 --> 00:10:40.200
“Organizations that protect mankind from
anomalous objects have existed all over the

00:10:40.200 --> 00:10:42.269
world for a long time.

00:10:42.269 --> 00:10:46.800
Although there have been numerous ways of
protection, it is indispensable to gain knowledge

00:10:46.800 --> 00:10:53.019
of anomalies, and the processes inevitably
include experiments using living human beings.

00:10:53.019 --> 00:10:57.809
This varies depending on times and regions
such as criminals, slaves, different ethnic

00:10:57.809 --> 00:10:59.679
groups and so on.

00:10:59.679 --> 00:11:04.259
Nevertheless, it still largely eroded the
minds of the researchers.

00:11:04.259 --> 00:11:07.999
Some people could not bear their sins and
threw themselves into the sea.

00:11:07.999 --> 00:11:12.529
Another person eliminated the distinction
between life and death to keep his peace of

00:11:12.529 --> 00:11:15.149
mind, but then he killed his family due to
a slight quarrel.

00:11:15.149 --> 00:11:20.860
I told many personnel repeatedly that these
sacrifices lead to protecting the world.

00:11:20.860 --> 00:11:25.740
Such words are, however, only so effective,
and many personnel’s hearts were understandably

00:11:25.740 --> 00:11:26.740
broken.”

00:11:26.740 --> 00:11:28.480
But I found that lamp.

00:11:28.480 --> 00:11:33.149
The circumstances were coincidental, but its
effect is noteworthy.

00:11:33.149 --> 00:11:37.459
Researchers have been able to study gruesome
anomalies without so much a care in the world.

00:11:37.459 --> 00:11:42.860
As a result, their work efficiency dramatically
increased, and the damage caused by the abnormities

00:11:42.860 --> 00:11:43.860
was soon negligible.

00:11:43.860 --> 00:11:48.360
Of course, the feelings remained, but there
were measures in place now.

00:11:48.360 --> 00:11:51.069
Amnestics, advanced anomalous therapies.

00:11:51.069 --> 00:11:55.699
If we wanted, we could remove the very emotion
of sadness from a man’s psyche.

00:11:55.699 --> 00:11:56.699
But we couldn’t.

00:11:56.699 --> 00:12:01.110
We’re already too far gone, and the lasting
effects of this lamp weigh on my mind the

00:12:01.110 --> 00:12:02.300
way it is.

00:12:02.300 --> 00:12:06.970
Those who abandoned their responsibility for
death by themselves became heterogeneous in

00:12:06.970 --> 00:12:10.009
daily life where general morality exists.

00:12:10.009 --> 00:12:13.699
They had become impossible to live among the
general public.”

00:12:13.699 --> 00:12:18.540
Institutions such as the Ethics Committee,
the board of directors responsible for maintaining

00:12:18.540 --> 00:12:23.499
the Foundation’s moral integrity, can only
do so much when surrounded by the amount of

00:12:23.499 --> 00:12:26.589
death the average secure facility deals with.

00:12:26.589 --> 00:12:30.170
This is likely why directives such as the
Acquittal protocol are greenlit.

00:12:30.170 --> 00:12:35.190
It doesn’t entirely negate the effects of
death, but it works pretty well as a cheap,

00:12:35.190 --> 00:12:39.610
simple, organization-wide amnestic, pushing
the uncomfortable memories and thoughts to

00:12:39.610 --> 00:12:44.649
the back of the mind until they’re absolutely
needed, and even then making sure to sugarcoat

00:12:44.649 --> 00:12:47.709
them in the most detached, robotic way possible.

00:12:47.709 --> 00:12:52.890
So, the next time you think you want to submit
a job application to the SCP Foundation, think

00:12:52.890 --> 00:13:00.119
of SCP-001, as well as all the other difficult,
immoral tasks you may be asked to perform.

00:13:00.119 --> 00:13:04.279
Is throwing away your humanity in service
of the goal of containment a noble one?

00:13:04.279 --> 00:13:09.829
What about changing the impact of one of life’s
most constant elements - death?

00:13:09.829 --> 00:13:14.920
Whatever the answer may be, there’s one
thing everyone can agree on - the life of

00:13:14.920 --> 00:13:21.379
Foundation personnel is not for everyone,
and it certainly isn’t easy on the mind.

00:13:21.379 --> 00:13:28.060
Now go check out “SCP-001 - The Way The
World Ends - Reality Unfolds” and “SCP-001

00:13:28.060 --> 00:13:29.519
- Which is the Real 001?

00:13:29.519 --> 00:13:34.499
(SCP Animation)” for more wicked and wild
SCP–001 possibilities!

